28
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:40,000
If anything, it just makes us afraid or confused or it's cynical or whatever, but it doesn't wake us up, encourage us, challenge us.

29
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
And what we want to do with this series is look at the book of Revelation the way that it was intended

30
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
and figure out what does it look like for us to keep these words of the prophecy.

31
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:56,000
So put some thought into that. Give Kent a hundred, he asked for 170 words or less of how this is stirring your mind

32
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
and your heart about how to keep it. I tried to do it in 170 words or less. It did not end well for me.

33
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Hopefully you do better than I do. Cool. All right. So we're going to be in just a minute after I pray,

34
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
we're going to get to the last cycle of sevens that we've been going through.

35
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
There's the seven seals, then the seven trumpets, and now the seven bowls.

36
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
And I just want to remind us, an important reminder this week, that these images are highly symbolic.

37
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Another problem with the way that oftentimes we try and interpret Revelation is we look for literal manifestations of these images on earth.

38
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:38,000
They're highly symbolic images. The example I like to give is like John saw Jesus as a lamb, though slain.

39
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:49,000
That is a true image of the person and character of Jesus. But do I think when we get to heaven that Jesus will look like a lamb slain, or will he have a resurrected, glorified body?

40
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
I think probably a resurrected, glorified body, but the symbol of the image is intended to communicate something to us.

41
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
The same with these seven seals and trumpets and bowls. There's lots of imagery and lots of stuff going on.

42
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:07,000
We should not be looking for a literal manifestation of this. The best illustration that I've seen in the commentaries,

43
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:14,000
do they talk about political cartoons? You guys into political cartoons? Anybody? Okay. Probably not.

44
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Some of you have never held a newspaper before, and it shows. It shows. Political cartoons, they have images of real figures,

45
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
but they're wildly dramatized and changed and adapted to try and get a point across.

46
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,000
And that's what John is doing with his images here in Revelation. He's giving you graphics and image for you to bring to mind,

47
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
but he's not asking you to look for literal manifestations of these things on earth.

48
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
He's asking you to see what is happening in the spiritual world behind the physical things on earth.

49
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Is that tracking? Okay. Hopefully it makes sense later. Let's pray. We'll get started.

50
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Father in heaven, we thank you for another opportunity to gather together to peel back the curtain on our experience of your kingdom on earth until you come.

51
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
God, there's a lot of challenging images in the book of Revelation.

52
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Probably the ones we're going to look at today rival the most challenging of the whole book.

53
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
And so I just pray that you would prepare our hearts and give us eyes to see what you actually want us to see.

54
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:30,000
That you would wake us up, encourage us, challenge us to put these words into practice.

55
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:37,000
God, so that your church would more clearly demonstrate your kingdom as it is in heaven on earth until you come.

56
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
In Jesus name I pray. Amen.

57
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Can you guys help me test some of my pet peeves out? I need a sanity check for my own anger issues.

58
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
So can we do a little crowd participation this morning?

59
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
I'm going to go through, you didn't even say yes.

60
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Okay. I don't want to assume. I'm not paying you all the hourly rate that I pay my therapist, but, I mean, I'll pay one hourly rate.

61
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,000
You guys can divvy it up, and maybe you'll get a quarter or something. It'll be great.

62
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Okay. So I'm going to give you some of the things that make me angry, and you tell me whether or not my responses are justified or whether or not I should probably just let it go. Okay?

63
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Somebody is going slow in the passing lane. Am I allowed to tailgate and high beam them?

64
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:47,000
You guys, okay. Or should I let it go? Okay, okay. Somebody in the grocery line in front of me has 18 items in the 10 item or less line.

65
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Do I get close to their back and say one, two, three? Or do I let it go? Okay.

66
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:06,000
This one really makes me mad. When people ride their bikes down a walking sidewalk, am I allowed to, like, give them a little juke?

67
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:15,000
And if they fall, they fall. If not, or should I let it go? You guys are bad influences.

68
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Last, this one Merson and I had recently at airport security. When every announcement leading up to me getting to the airport security tells me that I have to take my shoes off,

69
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:36,000
and I'm taking my shoes off, and the airport security person looks at me like I'm a moron and says, "You don't have to take your shoes off."

70
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Do I just slowly keep taking my shoes off, or do I let it go?

71
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:54,000
All right, I think most of these fall into the category of let it go and see a counselor, Ryan.

72
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:04,000
But, all right, now what if I found out that there was someone on our accounting team that was stealing money from the church offering?

73
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:11,000
What if I found out someone in our church was cheating on their spouse and hiding it?

74
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:22,000
What if I found out that Rory's daycare workers were abusing him?

75
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Do we feel the emotional shift in the room?

76
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:33,000
That's interesting to me because apparently anger isn't inherently good or bad.

77
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:42,000
A lot of it depends on what we're angry about and how we go about addressing that anger.

78
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Which calls out the tension that I think we're going to see as we hop into Revelation chapter 15 and 16.

79
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Revelation 15 and 16 is probably the place where we see this tension the loudest in the entire Scriptures.

80
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:11,000
We read about something that most modern people, including many Christians, including myself at many times in my life, are really uncomfortable talking about: the wrath of God.

81
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,000
And if we're going to understand what I think John wants us to understand in his symbolic imagery,

82
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:25,000
we're going to have to decide whether or not God's wrath is an embarrassing ancient Christian idea that we need to explain away.

83
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Or is it possible that we've misunderstood something important about God, his character, our sin, and his love for the world?

84
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:49,000
So I want to hop into these chapters. I want to pull that thread a little bit and see if it's possible that maybe we're kind of missing what the Scriptures want us to see in regards to God's wrath.

85
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Again, in chapters 15, 16, we're going to encounter this third set of sevens, and I know if you brought your friend to church this morning, you're like, "Seriously, Ryan?

86
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:09,000
I finally got him to come, and you're going to preach on the wrath of God?" Just give me like 30 minutes, and hopefully it'll make sense.

87
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:15,000
All right, chapter 15, 16, the third set of sevens we've been working through the past weeks, the seals, the trumpets, the bowls.

88
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And I'm not going to spend much time on the specifics of the seven bowls and what they are and what they mean, partially because most scholars believe that these three sets of sevens are a retelling and a, you guys want to learn a fancy Bible scholar word?

89
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Okay, recapitulation. Okay, that's what the scholars call it, a recapitulation.

90
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:47,000
It's John kind of sharing the same events from different angles, and he's crescendoing the intensification of them.

91
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:57,000
He's making them feel more weighty, more heavy, more dramatic in order to wake up the people of God and the people of the earth.

92
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:02,000
These seven bowls are going to parallel, in many ways, the plagues of Egypt during the Exodus.

93
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:10,000
So these people were steeped in the Old Testament Scriptures. They're immediately going to be inputting and downloading the story of the Exodus.

94
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,000
And I know for some of us, we can do that because we grew up in church and we went to Sunday school.

95
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Some of us maybe will struggle with some of that, but just know that in the backdrop of this story is the plagues of Egypt.

96
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:31,000
They were directed at the people of the earth. These bowls are just like the plagues were directed at the people of Egypt.

97
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:38,000
And Pharaoh who had hardened their hearts to God's warning and refused to repent of their wicked ways.

98
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:50,000
And interestingly enough, what we're going to see in the seven bowls is this is the first set of seven where it seems like the people of God are outside of the experience of the difficulty of what's going on.

99
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:58,000
The seven seals and the seven trumpets, the people of God, the faithful people of God are experiencing the difficulty and challenges.

100
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:03,000
They are crying out to God, "How long, O Lord, will you allow these things to happen?"

101
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:11,000
And when we get to chapter 15 and 16, we're going to see that the people of God are actually outside the experience of the wrath of God.

102
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Let's just hop in and see if I can make this make sense in the next 30 minutes.

103
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Okay. I've been struggling, just so you know. Okay. Real people realize.

104
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:37,000
Chapter 15, verse 1, "I saw in heaven another great and marvelous sign, seven angels with seven last plagues, last because with them God's wrath is," say it with me, "completed.

105
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:42,000
And I saw what looked like a sea of glass glowing with fire and standing beside the sea."

106
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
NIV translates it beside the sea. I don't like that translation.

107
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:52,000
The literal Greek is actually on the sea. The people of God are standing on the sea.

108
00:13:52,000 --> 00:14:01,000
That should communicate something to us. Again, we've talked about how waters throughout the time of the early readers, they represented chaos and death and hardship.

109
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:07,000
And now God's people are standing on top of the sea of chaos, which is intended to represent death and chaos.

110
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:13,000
They are standing victorious and conquering over that chaos. I saw them standing on the sea of glass.

111
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Those who had been victorious over the beast and its image and over the number of its name, they held harps given to them by God.

112
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:28,000
They sang song of God's servant Moses and of the lamb. They said this, "Great and marvelous are your deeds, Lord God Almighty.

113
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Just and true are your ways, King of all the nations, who will not fear you, Lord, and bring glory to your name, for you are holy.

114
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:44,000
All the nations will come and worship before you, for your righteous acts have been revealed."

115
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:50,000
And then we turn to chapter 16 and we read this in verse 5, "Then I heard the angel in charge of the waters,

116
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:55,000
the waters that the believers are standing on, say, 'You are just in these judgments, O holy one,

117
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:01,000
you who are and who were, for they have shed the blood, the people of earth have shed the blood of your holy people

118
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:07,000
and your prophets, and you have given them blood to drink as they deserve.' And I heard the altar respond,

119
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:14,000
'Yes, Lord Almighty, true and just are your judgments.'"

120
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:19,000
So here we see the faithful people of God, not just passing through the waters of chaos,

121
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:25,000
but literally standing victoriously on the waters of chaos, singing a worship song to the Lamb,

122
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:36,000
who has revealed the righteousness of God, the justice and grace, the judgment and mercy of God in His person and work and character.

123
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:41,000
And we hear the altar saying, "True and just are your judgments."

124
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:48,000
We could input the word wrath there, "True and just is your wrath."

125
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,000
Wrath, which leads me to the first point as we kind of pull this thread,

126
00:15:52,000 --> 00:16:00,000
this tension that we're feeling about God's anger towards sin and the chaos and destruction the dragon is bringing the earth.

127
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,000
My first point here is that God's wrath is good because God is good.

128
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,000
And again, most of us do not like the idea of wrath until we think about the alternative,

129
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:17,000
until we think about someone cheating on their spouse and hiding it and me as a person

130
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:22,000
knowing about it and doing nothing. So we think about a vulnerable child being abused by those

131
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:27,000
who are supposed to be taking care of them and somebody who has a position to stop it doing nothing.

132
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:31,000
When we think about the alternative of righteous anger not existing,

133
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:38,000
what would it be for an all-powerful and loving God to ignore the injustice of a broken world?

134
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:43,000
The evil that is influenced by the dragon and seeks to distort God's good creation,

135
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:52,000
the evil that is intent on taking what God created for life in abundance and using it to steal, kill, and destroy.

136
00:16:52,000 --> 00:17:02,000
I love this scholar, Leon Morris, how he defines God's wrath. He says, "God's wrath is a strong and settled opposition to all that is evil."

137
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:11,000
We could expand that definition to say a strong and settled opposition to all that seeks to destroy God's good creation.

138
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:27,000
The same reason the Israelites could sing the song of Moses after watching the Red Sea swallow the Egyptian army is the same reason the faithful people of God can rejoice at God's wrath being poured out on the unrepentant inhabitants of the earth.

139
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,000
Because we know sufficient warning has been given, a sufficient way out has been offered.

140
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:44,000
And yet many people remain unmoved in partnership with the dragon and his schemes to destroy God's good world through their sinful actions.

141
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:59,000
So if we really think about the weight of sin and the way it impacts creation, if we understand the weight of God's love for his creation and his desire for it to produce life and flourishing and we look all around us.

142
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And we've talked about this for the past few weeks, the way in which people of the earth partner with the dragon to take what was created for good and to distort it for evil.

143
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:16,000
Then we can understand how the people of God who have cried out, "How long, O Lord, will you allow this to happen?"

144
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:26,000
That when judgment and wrath is finally poured out to its completion, you can understand how the people of God could take a sigh of relief and say, "True and just are your ways."

145
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:35,000
It's not that we're happy about wrath. It's that we realize the result of letting sin go unchecked.

146
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:44,000
I don't think any good, faithful follower of Jesus would be in their bones excited about God pouring out judgment.

147
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:51,000
But we understand the alternative. We understand that the God who pours out this justice is good.

148
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:59,000
I love how N.T. Wright illustrates it in his short commentary on Revelation. I'm paraphrasing because I didn't want to quote two pages.

149
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:05,000
Actually, I did want to quote two pages, but I felt like there might be some copyright issues there.

150
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:11,000
He says this, "Imagine a small village on the outskirts of town where injustice keeps piling up.

151
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:18,000
A worker is cheated, a widow is stolen from, a family is exploited, and someone is falsely accused.

152
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:21,000
But there's no one around with the authority to make it right.

153
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:27,000
Finally, after months of building frustration and hopelessness, the district judge makes his way to the town.

154
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:35,000
He will hear each case properly and fairly. He will steadfastly refuse all bribes, and he will make his judgment.

155
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:46,000
For the oppressor, that's bad news. But for everyone who's been suffering and even dying under the weight of unchecked injustice, it's wonderful news.

156
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Wright says, "Now take that village and magnify it up to the global level," and this is what we're talking about when we talk about the biblical idea of judgment.

157
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:05,000
God, through Jesus, comes to confront all that is destroying his good creation, and he intends to put all things to right.

158
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:13,000
I think when we begin to realize the level of injustice and death that is brought to the world by the dragon and all who wear his mark,

159
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:17,000
and the level of love and holiness contained in the character of God,

160
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:23,000
we can begin to say with the altar, "True and just are your judgments."

161
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:36,000
Do we understand? Do we catch glimpses of the beauty of God's creation and the goodness of sacrificial love poured out to the vulnerable?

162
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Do we see the goodness of God's creation? And do we see all the areas all over the world

163
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:50,000
where it is being attacked and taken advantage of, and where people who are vulnerable are being used for their ability to create wealth,

164
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:57,000
and then those who are abusing them are taking advantage of that. And we can see the ways in which leaving this unchecked

165
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:08,000
would not be good for a God who is good and loved. God's wrath is good because God is good and because evil exists.

166
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:16,000
The second thing I want to point out from these two chapters is that God desires, his desire is repentance, not rejection.

167
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:22,000
That the reason we don't get the wrath of God until chapter 15 is because John is trying to demonstrate the thing that God

168
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:28,000
has been trying to demonstrate to people throughout all creation, and that God is wanting a relationship with his people,

169
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that he's inviting all creation into right relationship with him, that he's given them a free way out of his wrath and his judgment,

170
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,000
and that he's inviting them to participate in the goodness of his creation.

171
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:47,000
God is slow and patient and kind, and he does not desire repentance, or he does desire repentance, not rejection,

172
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:54,000
and yet he is met over and over again with rejection from the people of the earth. Verse 9, they respond.

173
00:21:54,000 --> 00:22:00,000
It says, "But they refused to repent and glorify God." Again, he's pouring out this judgment. He's pouring out these warnings.

174
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:04,000
He's trying to get their attention, and they refused to repent and to glorify him.

175
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:17,000
Again, in verse 11, they refused to repent of what they had done. And then in verse 21, they cursed God on account of the plagues because of how terrible they were.

176
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:24,000
And I think Pastor Sam, a few weeks ago, did such a great job unpacking this idea in the message of the seven trumpets.

177
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:33,000
The point of John's intensifying symbols is to show that God is desperately trying to get the attention of those who have been deceived and coerced into submission to the dragon.

178
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:43,000
And his counterfeit kingdom. That the intensification of the warnings is God's love for these people who have been deceived and coerced.

179
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:55,000
He's trying to get them to see there's a better way. Like a loving father who's pleading with his son to give up the behaviors that are killing him and his family and those around him.

180
00:22:55,000 --> 00:23:02,000
And there is a real tension here where maybe some of you in the room are like, "Ryan, I don't struggle with the tension of God's wrath.

181
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:13,000
The thing that I struggle with is the tension of why it's taking him so long." Some of you who have been used and abused and taken advantage of, some of you have had horrific things done to you.

182
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:24,000
And you see the people who did the horrific things to you, and you see them seemingly flourishing, and you see you dealing with the consequences of the actions that they did against you.

183
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:31,000
And your question to me and to the Bible is not, "Why does God have wrath?" Your question is, "Why is his wrath taking so long?"

184
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:41,000
And the answer to that question is that God is a loving and patient Father.

185
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:47,000
2 Peter frames it up this way. This is Eugene Peterson's quotation, or his translation of it.

186
00:23:47,000 --> 00:24:00,000
It says this, "Don't overlook the obvious here, friends. With God, one day is as good as a thousand years, and a thousand years as a day. God isn't late with his promise, as some measure lateness.

187
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:09,000
He is restraining himself on account of you, holding back the end because he doesn't want anyone lost. He's giving everyone space and time to change.

188
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:21,000
But when the day of God's judgment does come, it will be unannounced like a thief. The sky will collapse with a thunderous bang and everything disintegrating in a raging inferno.

189
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:29,000
The earth and all its work exposed to the scrutiny of judgment. God is slow and patient and kind.

190
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:36,000
He does not want any to perish, but to all would experience life and life everlasting.

191
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:46,000
He has a deep desire for every human being created in his image to understand what life and life more abundantly looks like.

192
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:53,000
So whichever side of the wrath tension you struggle with, the Scripture is intent on you seeing God's goodness in the middle of it.

193
00:24:53,000 --> 00:25:00,000
If you struggle with there being wrath at all, the Scripture wants you to think about the alternative.

194
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:06,000
What would a world look like where a just, good, loving God lets sin go unpunished?

195
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:15,000
And again, when we hear sin, oftentimes we think about the ticky-tacky things that maybe a religious community put their thumb on us for.

196
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:28,000
But we need to zoom out and see the ways in which our participation with sin and sin kind of spreading throughout creation actually causes death and destruction for people all over the place.

197
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:39,000
So for those of us that struggle with wrath, we need to understand that God is just so patient and kind.

198
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:47,000
And for those of us that are just like, "Yeah, He's not doing it soon enough. I need it now. I need relief now. I need justice now."

199
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:56,000
We need to see God's patience and His goodness and see His patience with us and want that experience for those who don't yet have it.

200
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:04,000
The tragedy of Revelation 15 and 16 isn't God's unwillingness to save, but humanity's unwillingness to receive that salvation.

201
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:17,000
This is the tragedy that God is continually extending salvation, offering forgiveness and grace, and humanity is looking the other way and saying, "We're good."

202
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:28,000
God's righteous wrath is an outpouring of His love for people and His desire for the redemption of His creation.

203
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:34,000
Again, Peterson's translation of a famous verse that we like to quote, John 3:16,

204
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:39,000
but if we carry it through, it gives us some real clarity here. John 3:16-19, it says,

205
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:44,000
"This is how much God loved the world. He gave His Son, His one and only Son,

206
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:52,000
and this is why, so that no one need be destroyed. By believing in Him, anyone can have whole and lasting life."

207
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:58,000
God didn't go to all the trouble of sending His Son to merely point an accusing finger, telling the world how bad it was.

208
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:03,000
He came to help, to put the world right again. Anyone who trusts in Him is acquitted.

209
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:09,000
Anyone who refuses to trust Him has long since been under the death sentence without knowing it.

210
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:16,000
And why? Because of that person's failure to believe in the one-of-a-kind Son of God when introduced to Him.

211
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:21,000
This is the crisis we're in. This is the tension we're experiencing.

212
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:26,000
God light streamed into the world, but men and women everywhere ran for the darkness.

213
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:32,000
This is the tension we're in. God brought light into the world through His Son, Jesus,

214
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:40,000
and the world crucified Him. This is human sin's response to God's goodness.

215
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:44,000
Kill and run away. Run from the light into the darkness.

216
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:52,000
And what is a holy, righteous, and loving God to do with a people who literally crucified His Son,

217
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:57,000
the perfect image of His goodness and love and forgiveness?

218
00:27:57,000 --> 00:28:06,000
And they didn't crucify Him because of His harshness. They crucified Him because their desire to be in control.

219
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:16,000
In Jesus, we see God's love collide with humanity's sin. His crucifixion demonstrates our love of darkness rather than light.

220
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:23,000
God's wrath is inevitable because of His goodness and the evil that our sin perpetuates.

221
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:30,000
But His desire is repentance, not rejection. He is slow and patient and kind.

222
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:37,000
And He is trying to get our attention to see His goodness in the midst of wrath.

223
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:47,000
You guys hanging in there? All right, good. Well, it's going to get, it gets better starting now, I think.

224
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:55,000
The third point I want to emphasize here from Revelation 15 and 16 is that God's wrath will come to an end.

225
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:05,000
And these chapters are, I think, the most important thing that we can do when interpreting these two chapters is see the phrases that they're bracketed by.

226
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:14,000
Again, this is a new scene that starts in 15 and ends at the end of 16, and it starts with these words, "God's wrath is completed."

227
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:24,000
As the bowls are being poured out, the declaration is that God's wrath is coming to an end, and then the chapters end in chapter 16, verse 17.

228
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:33,000
"The seventh angel poured out his bowl, the last bowl, into the air, and out of the temple came a loud voice from the throne saying, 'It is done.'"

229
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:41,000
And if we've read the Gospels, this should be triggering something in our minds and in our hearts.

230
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:50,000
We should be thinking about the crucifixion scene. We should be thinking about the death of Christ, where Jesus, hanging on the cross, declared, "It is finished."

231
00:29:50,000 --> 00:30:00,000
The work of God's eternal redemption plan for all creation was completed on that blessed and Good Friday.

232
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:09,000
Our Lord rested from his eternal work on Saturday, was raised on Sunday. A few weeks later, ascended to his rightful place on the throne,

233
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:14,000
where he sounds like a lion who is roaring, but he looks like a lamb who has been slain.

234
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:18,000
The work of Jesus is the culmination of God's kingdom.

235
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:27,000
The redemption of God solidified. Revelation is showing us what must happen between the inauguration and consummation of that kingdom.

236
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:32,000
It's not pretty because the dragon is flailing. Remember a couple weeks ago, the dragon was thrown.

237
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:38,000
Okay, you were better last week, not terrible this week. I didn't really set you up super good either.

238
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:48,000
The dragon was thrown. He has ultimately been defeated, but he is still flailing and causing chaos and destruction.

239
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:59,000
And it's in this in-between time of sin ultimately coming to its culmination in the crucifixion of the image of God.

240
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:07,000
And God's patiently waiting for all people to come to repentance. It's in this in-between stage.

241
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:15,000
Where we deal with this tension of God having wrath, or how long is God's wrath going to take?

242
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:22,000
And it's in these chapters that we see the good news that God's wrath will come to an end.

243
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:28,000
If I had to summarize it, I'd say it this way. Before sin existed, God was love.

244
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Because sin exists, God has wrath towards evil. And when final, and when evil is finally dealt with,

245
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:41,000
and every tear is wiped away from our eyes, God will still be love.

246
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:51,000
My main point for this entire message, the thing that I was struggling to land on because the weight of talking about God's wrath is so heavy,

247
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:57,000
my main point is this, that God's wrath will come to an end because his redemption will win.

248
00:31:57,000 --> 00:32:02,000
This is the story of the book of Revelation. Just wait till we get to the last chapter.

249
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:06,000
Again, every tear is wiped from our eyes. There's no more mourning or sorrow or pain.

250
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:10,000
This is where the story is headed. But in between the crucifixion of Jesus,

251
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:15,000
the culmination of sin, death, and the grave showing its ugly head to the world,

252
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:18,000
crucifying an innocent savior.

253
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:30,000
It's between that moment and his return that we deal with this tension where we're waiting for his final redemption to come.

254
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:38,000
And in that, there is some experience of wrath, but God's wrath will come to an end because his redemption will win.

255
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:42,000
So let's revisit our therapy session from this morning.

256
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:54,000
I don't think the tension that we feel around the wrath of God is actually that we have a problem with holy and just and righteous judgment.

257
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:10,000
I don't think we have a problem with someone in a position of authority and morality executing judgment on somebody who's causing death and chaos to vulnerable people.

258
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:19,000
I think the issue that we have is we don't have an accurate view of our sinful actions and how it partners with the dragon to destroy God's good world.

259
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:27,000
We think our sin is not that big of a deal. I love C.S. Lewis has this illustration where he talks about the God that we want to create in our own image

260
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:35,000
is like a playful grandfather who winks at the grandson taking the fourth or fifth piece of candy, turning his eye.

261
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:44,000
Like we just want this God who doesn't really take seriously the way that we're behaving and leading to our own death and destruction.

262
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:53,000
We think our sin is not that big of a deal. We compare ourselves to others. We think other people deserve wrath, but we deserve grace.

263
00:33:54,000 --> 00:34:04,000
If you don't believe me, just subscribe to this thing called "What's Happening Winchester in Frederick County" on Facebook.

264
00:34:04,000 --> 00:34:07,000
See, some of you know.

265
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:13,000
There's this thing that happens, and it's so revealing to me as a pastor,

266
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:20,000
because somebody will post on there an article of somebody locally that's done something horrible and bad

267
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:30,000
and worthy of some consequences and punishment, and just the comments will be flooded with the most righteous people who have never done anything wrong ever.

268
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:39,000
Not wanting the system of justice to be played out for these individuals, but wanting them to be taken behind sheds and...

269
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:44,000
Right? Like, horrible, horrible things are said about these people.

270
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:53,000
Because that's in our heart of hearts, that's how we deal with our own guilt, our own judgment, our own sin.

271
00:34:53,000 --> 00:35:00,000
We downplay the sin that's in our hearts, and we look and find somebody outside of us that we can point to and say, they're the ones that are deserving of wrath.

272
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:05,000
They're the ones that are deserving, that deserve judgment.

273
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:13,000
This is part of the thrust behind the Sermon on the Mount, where Jesus is saying to us, "You think adultery should be judged, but not lust."

274
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:23,000
And Jesus is saying, "Lust kills, steals, and destroys relationships and marriages and families just as bad as adultery does."

275
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:33,000
And you want to downplay your sinful actions that you hide in the darkness so that you can point to somebody whose sin has drug them into the light and say, "They deserve judgment. I deserve mercy."

276
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:39,000
You think murder should be judged, but not unforgiveness, gossip, or slander.

277
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:43,000
And Jesus is saying, "These things cause just as much damage and chaos."

278
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:50,000
And as a matter of fact, sometimes even worse because you can hide them and perpetuate them in the darkness for years and years and years.

279
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:53,000
Chaos and destruction behind you.

280
00:35:53,000 --> 00:35:58,000
Jesus says, "You see the speck in your neighbor's eye and you have a four by four in yours."

281
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:23,000
I think, as I was wrestling and praying over this this week, I think God's wrath begins to become easier for us to handle when we realize the chaos and destruction that is created when we run from the light and hide in the darkness.

282
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:36,000
And if we're seriously honest with ourselves, all of us can bring to mind and heart things that we do, attitudes and actions and behaviors that we're hiding in the darkness,

283
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:42,000
that we've justified and explained away and said, "Don't ultimately cause anybody any harm."

284
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:50,000
And we know. We know that gossip and slander isn't victimless.

285
00:36:50,000 --> 00:37:00,000
We know what our actions are doing. We know, and even if we don't, they are hardening our hearts towards God's righteousness.

286
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:08,000
They're hardening our hearts towards what good creation is intended to look like, what sacrificial love should look like in our relationships.

287
00:37:08,000 --> 00:37:16,000
We run from the light. We hide in the darkness.

288
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:30,000
And so we have a problem with God's wrath because we have a misunderstanding of our sin and our participation with the dragon and his counterfeit kingdom.

289
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:50,000
I love how C.S. Lewis puts it. He says, "The more God loves you, the more determined he must be to pull you back from your way, which leads nowhere, into his way, which leads you where you want to go, to him."

290
00:37:53,000 --> 00:38:02,000
This is why we must be prepared to find God implacably and immovably forbidding what may seem to us very small and trivial things.

291
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:06,000
Again, we do the comparison game. Our sin is small, other people's sin is big.

292
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:20,000
But as we understand God's love for us and his hatred towards the way in which sin destroys his good world, we can see the smaller things being pulled out.

293
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:37,000
And this is why, if you've ever had a relationship with somebody who's walked with the Lord for 50 years, and to you they look like the godliest person you've ever met, and you hear them talk and they're like, "I am the worst person you've ever met."

294
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:43,000
Because the more you walk with the Lord, the more your life is exposed to his light.

295
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:56,000
And the more you realize things that in your past you would have called small and insignificant, things that don't really impact anybody negatively, the more aware you become of how they destroy your soul and your relationships and God's creation.

296
00:38:56,000 --> 00:39:03,000
And you see this progression even in the life of the Apostle Paul.

297
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:12,000
In one of Paul's first letters, he calls himself an apostle, right?

298
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:19,000
New believer had this mountaintop experience with God. God showed up in a flash of light, blinded him, healed him.

299
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:24,000
He's like, "God must really love me. I'm an apostle."

300
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:34,000
A few years later, he writes another letter, and he refers to himself as the least of the apostles.

301
00:39:34,000 --> 00:39:45,000
He's walking with the Lord, and he's starting to understand the way in which his sin negatively impacts creation and his relationships all around him.

302
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:55,000
And the last letter that Paul writes, he refers to himself as the chief of sinners.

303
00:39:55,000 --> 00:40:08,000
See the progression? As he walks with the Lord more and more, he realizes the weight of his sin and the negative impact it has on the world around him.

304
00:40:09,000 --> 00:40:22,000
I think this is where the tension lies with us when it comes to the wrath of God. Do we fully understand how good and how loving and how good God's creation would be if sin didn't exist?

305
00:40:22,000 --> 00:40:32,000
And do we fully understand our sin? Not just those who are, we can easily find a sin outside of us, where we can say that's causing death, chaos, and destruction.

306
00:40:33,000 --> 00:40:46,000
But the sin inside of us, which leads me to the final question I want us to meditate on this morning. If God intends to destroy evil, what about the evil inside of me?

307
00:40:46,000 --> 00:40:58,000
I think, yeah. The lust, the hatred, the unforgiveness, the greed, the idolatry, the pride, insert your issue here.

308
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:03,000
All of this contributes to the destruction of God's good creation.

309
00:41:03,000 --> 00:41:12,000
And based on God's eternally wise and perfect redemption plan, it is all deserving of wrath.

310
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:22,000
In the Old Testament, the prophets talk about this wrath like a cup, God's cup being poured out on evil.

311
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:38,000
And the night Jesus was portrayed, he prayed, "Father, if possible, let this cup pass from me.

312
00:41:38,000 --> 00:41:41,000
Nevertheless, not my will, but yours be done."

313
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:50,000
And just before he prayed, with this imagery of the cup of wrath being poured out, just before he prayed, "God, let this cup pass from me."

314
00:41:51,000 --> 00:41:55,000
He offered his disciples a different cup.

315
00:41:55,000 --> 00:42:00,000
He called it the cup of the covenant in his blood.

316
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:05,000
Then he goes to the cross to drink the cup of wrath.

317
00:42:05,000 --> 00:42:12,000
So what about the evil inside of you?

318
00:42:12,000 --> 00:42:14,000
What about the evil inside of me?

319
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:22,000
I think the Scriptures give us the picture that all sin will be dealt with.

320
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:30,000
That God's wrath will come to its completion, and his love will continue to exist for all eternity.

321
00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:35,000
I think there's two cups that that wrath finds its completion in.

322
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:41,000
It's the cup of wrath, and it's the cup of the covenant in Christ's blood.

323
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:45,000
So is that something that you have received?

324
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:52,000
Have you accepted the grace and forgiveness of Jesus on your behalf?

325
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:56,000
Have you done what the people in Revelation 15 and 16 have yet to do?

326
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:57,000
Have you repented?

327
00:42:57,000 --> 00:43:02,000
Have you acknowledged the ways in which you've contributed to the destruction of God's good world?

328
00:43:02,000 --> 00:43:07,000
Have you turned from the cup of wrath and moved towards the cup of the covenant in Christ's blood?

329
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:17,000
A day will come, and it will not be announced, as we read in 2 Peter.

330
00:43:17,000 --> 00:43:22,000
It will come like a thief in the night. A day will come when the last of God's wrath will be poured out.

331
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:29,000
And between now and then, there is a cup that sits on the table of churches all across the world.

332
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:34,000
The cup of the new covenant in His blood at the communion table.

333
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:39,000
It's an open invitation to all who would acknowledge their sin before a holy God

334
00:43:39,000 --> 00:43:43,000
and trust the atoning work of Christ on their behalf.

